# Phase 7 — Next.js Split-Screen Reader & Chat UI

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a dual-pane interface in Next.js 15: an interactive PDF document reader on the left and an AI chat interface on the right. When users click an inline citation pill (`[Page 3]`) in the chat response, the PDF reader automatically scrolls to and highlights that referenced page and text passage.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

A verified RAG platform must enable zero-friction fact verification. By coupling streaming chat tokens to page anchors in `react-pdf`, users click citations to inspect the source material in context without losing their reading flow.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Next.js Split-Screen Reader Component
In `components/SplitRAGViewer.tsx`:
```tsx
"use client";
import { useState } from "react";

export function SplitRAGViewer() {
  const [currentPage, setCurrentPage] = useState(1);
  const [messages, setMessages] = useState<any[]>([]);

  const handleCitationClick = (page: number) => {
    setCurrentPage(page);
  };

  return (
    <div className="grid grid-cols-2 h-screen bg-[#0a0a0a] text-neutral-200">
      {/* Left: PDF Document Viewer */}
      <div className="border-r border-[#262626] p-6 overflow-y-auto">
        <div className="flex items-center justify-between mb-4">
          <span className="text-sm font-semibold">Active Document Viewer</span>
          <span className="text-xs bg-[#262626] px-2 py-1 rounded">Page {currentPage}</span>
        </div>
        <div className="bg-[#141414] border border-[#262626] rounded-lg p-6 min-h-[500px]">
          <p className="text-sm text-neutral-400">Rendering document content for page {currentPage}...</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Right: AI Streaming Chat */}
      <div className="flex flex-col h-full p-6">
        <h2 className="text-lg font-bold mb-4">Document Intelligence</h2>
        <div className="flex-1 overflow-y-auto space-y-4">
          <div className="bg-[#141414] p-4 rounded-lg border border-[#262626]">
            The maintenance timeout limit is set to 45 seconds{" "}
            <button
              onClick={() => handleCitationClick(4)}
              className="text-xs bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono hover:bg-amber-500/30"
            >
              [Page 4]
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] Split-screen renders PDF viewport alongside interactive chat panel
- [ ] Clicking `[Page X]` citation pill scrolls the left viewport directly to target page

# Phase 2 — Styling the Terminal with Chalk, Boxen & Gradient Typography

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Transform bland terminal output into a sleek, modern visual interface. Render rounded borders and padded container boxes using `boxen`, styled ANSI themes with `chalk`, and a colorful gradient greeting header with `gradient-string`.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

Developers spend hours in terminal environments. Clean visual hierarchy, consistent color token palettes (Emerald for success, Amber for warnings, Rose for errors), and formatted ASCII containers improve scannability and user experience.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Terminal Theme Utility
In `src/ui/theme.ts`:
```typescript
import chalk from "chalk";
import boxen from "boxen";

export const theme = {
  success: chalk.hex("#10b981").bold,
  warning: chalk.hex("#f59e0b").bold,
  error: chalk.hex("#ef4444").bold,
  info: chalk.hex("#06b6d4"),
  dim: chalk.hex("#737373"),
  highlight: chalk.hex("#f97316").bold
};

export function renderBanner(title: string, subtitle: string) {
  const content = `${theme.highlight(title)}\n${theme.dim(subtitle)}`;
  console.log(
    boxen(content, {
      padding: 1,
      margin: 1,
      borderStyle: "round",
      borderColor: "#f97316"
    })
  );
}
```

### Step 2 · Attach Banner to CLI Boot
In `src/index.ts`:
```typescript
import { renderBanner } from "./ui/theme";

program.hook("preAction", () => {
  renderBanner("⚡ DEVFLOW CLI", "Developer Task & Automation Engine");
});
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] Running `devflow` displays a formatted rounded terminal box with amber accent borders
- [ ] Output strings utilize consistent color palettes across information levels

# Phase 3 — Interactive User Prompts with Inquirer & Autocomplete

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Construct interactive, keyboard-driven terminal wizards using Inquirer. You will build prompts for task creation, single-choice priority pickers (`Low`, `Medium`, `High`, `Urgent`), multi-select tag selectors, and validated input fields.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

Instead of forcing users to memorize long CLI command arguments, interactive prompts guide users step-by-step with arrow-key navigation and inline validation rules that provide instant error feedback.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Interactive Task Creation Wizard
In `src/prompts/taskPrompt.ts`:
```typescript
import inquirer from "inquirer";

export interface TaskInput {
  title: string;
  category: string;
  priority: "LOW" | "MEDIUM" | "HIGH" | "URGENT";
  tags: string[];
}

export async function promptCreateTask(): Promise<TaskInput> {
  const answers = await inquirer.prompt([
    {
      type: "input",
      name: "title",
      message: "Task description:",
      validate: (input: string) => {
        if (!input.trim()) return "Task title cannot be empty.";
        if (input.length < 3) return "Title must be at least 3 characters long.";
        return true;
      }
    },
    {
      type: "list",
      name: "priority",
      message: "Select priority level:",
      choices: ["LOW", "MEDIUM", "HIGH", "URGENT"],
      default: "MEDIUM"
    },
    {
      type: "checkbox",
      name: "tags",
      message: "Assign tags:",
      choices: ["backend", "frontend", "devops", "database", "testing", "docs"]
    }
  ]);

  return answers;
}
```

### Step 2 · Register Add Command
In `src/commands/add.ts`:
```typescript
import { promptCreateTask } from "../prompts/taskPrompt";
import { theme } from "../ui/theme";

export async function addCommand() {
  const input = await promptCreateTask();
  console.log(theme.success(`✔ Task "${input.title}" configured with [${input.priority}] priority.`));
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] `devflow add` triggers the interactive wizard with arrow navigation
- [ ] Submitting empty input prompts inline error without terminating process
- [ ] Checkboxes allow toggling multiple tags with Spacebar and confirming with Enter

# Phase 4 — Persistent Storage Engine: Local JSON & Config Management

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build a local persistent storage engine. Store developer tasks, global preferences, and session tokens in `~/.devflow/tasks.json` in the user's home directory. Implement atomic file writes to prevent data corruption during abrupt terminal exits.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

Storing configuration inside the NPM project folder fails when the package is upgraded or executed via `npx`. By writing persistent data to `os.homedir()`, data survives across sessions, directory changes, and system reboots. We use atomic write patterns (`write to temp file then rename`) to prevent partial file writes.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Storage Engine Manager
In `src/storage/db.ts`:
```typescript
import fs from "fs";
import path from "path";
import os from "os";

export interface StoredTask {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  priority: string;
  tags: string[];
  completed: boolean;
  createdAt: string;
}

const STORAGE_DIR = path.join(os.homedir(), ".devflow");
const DB_FILE = path.join(STORAGE_DIR, "tasks.json");

export class StorageEngine {
  private static ensureDir() {
    if (!fs.existsSync(STORAGE_DIR)) {
      fs.mkdirSync(STORAGE_DIR, { recursive: true });
    }
    if (!fs.existsSync(DB_FILE)) {
      fs.writeFileSync(DB_FILE, JSON.stringify([], null, 2));
    }
  }

  static getTasks(): StoredTask[] {
    this.ensureDir();
    const raw = fs.readFileSync(DB_FILE, "utf-8");
    return JSON.parse(raw);
  }

  static saveTask(task: StoredTask) {
    const tasks = this.getTasks();
    tasks.push(task);
    this.atomicSave(tasks);
  }

  static markCompleted(id: string): boolean {
    const tasks = this.getTasks();
    const task = tasks.find((t) => t.id === id);
    if (!task) return false;
    task.completed = true;
    this.atomicSave(tasks);
    return true;
  }

  private static atomicSave(data: any) {
    this.ensureDir();
    const tempFile = `${DB_FILE}.tmp.${Date.now()}`;
    fs.writeFileSync(tempFile, JSON.stringify(data, null, 2), "utf-8");
    fs.renameSync(tempFile, DB_FILE);
  }
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] Creating a task persists it to `~/.devflow/tasks.json`
- [ ] Task records survive terminal reboots and window closes
- [ ] Atomic file write eliminates corrupt JSON states

# Phase 5 — Async API Fetching & Animated Spinners with Ora

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Implement asynchronous external data fetching with smooth, animated terminal spinners using `ora`. You will build a remote synchronization command (`devflow sync`) that contacts external APIs, handles network timeouts gracefully, and updates terminal spinner states.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

Long-running asynchronous network calls in CLIs can make users assume the application has frozen. Animated terminal spinners keep users informed. If an operation succeeds, the spinner morphs into a green checkmark; on network failure, it turns into a red cross with helpful diagnostics.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Async Cloud Sync Worker with Ora
In `src/commands/sync.ts`:
```typescript
import ora from "ora";
import { theme } from "../ui/theme";

export async function syncCommand() {
  const spinner = ora({
    text: "Contacting remote DevFlow synchronization gateway...",
    color: "cyan"
  }).start();

  try {
    // Simulate cloud round-trip
    await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 2000));
    spinner.text = "Synchronizing local tasks with cloud workspace...";

    await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1500));
    spinner.succeed(theme.success("Workspace synchronized successfully! (4 tasks synced)"));
  } catch (err: any) {
    spinner.fail(theme.error(`Sync failed: ${err.message}`));
  }
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] `devflow sync` animates a smooth braille spinner during async latency
- [ ] Successful network resolution displays green checkmark confirmation
- [ ] Timeout errors display clear failure messages with red indicator

# Phase 6 — List & Complete Commands: Beautiful Terminal Tables

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Implement `devflow list` and `devflow done <id>` commands. You will format tasks into aligned ASCII terminal tables using `cli-table3`, with color-coded status badges and priority tags.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

Terminal tables must auto-fit terminal window widths (`process.stdout.columns`) and handle multi-line strings cleanly. Formatting data into aligned tables with distinct status columns allows users to digest hundreds of items in seconds.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Formatted Table Output
Install `cli-table3`:
```bash
npm install cli-table3
```

In `src/commands/list.ts`:
```typescript
import Table from "cli-table3";
import { StorageEngine } from "../storage/db";
import { theme } from "../ui/theme";

export function listCommand() {
  const tasks = StorageEngine.getTasks();
  if (tasks.length === 0) {
    console.log(theme.dim("No tasks found. Run `devflow add` to create one!"));
    return;
  }

  const table = new Table({
    head: [theme.highlight("ID"), theme.highlight("Title"), theme.highlight("Priority"), theme.highlight("Status")],
    colWidths: [10, 35, 12, 12]
  });

  tasks.forEach((t) => {
    const statusText = t.completed ? theme.success("✔ Done") : theme.warning("○ Pending");
    table.push([t.id.slice(0, 8), t.title, t.priority, statusText]);
  });

  console.log(table.toString());
}
```

### Step 2 · Complete Task Command
In `src/commands/done.ts`:
```typescript
import { StorageEngine } from "../storage/db";
import { theme } from "../ui/theme";

export function doneCommand(idPrefix: string) {
  const tasks = StorageEngine.getTasks();
  const match = tasks.find((t) => t.id.startsWith(idPrefix));

  if (!match) {
    console.log(theme.error(`No task found matching prefix: ${idPrefix}`));
    return;
  }

  StorageEngine.markCompleted(match.id);
  console.log(theme.success(`✔ Marked task [${match.title}] as completed!`));
}
```

---

## ✅ Phase Checkpoint

- [ ] `devflow list` prints aligned ASCII table with styled status indicators
- [ ] `devflow done <id>` marks task completed and updates persistent JSON file

# Phase 7 — Automated CLI Testing with Vitest

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Write automated unit and integration tests for CLI commands using Vitest. You will mock the Node.js filesystem (`fs`) to prevent polluting the actual user home directory during tests and verify task lifecycle transitions.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

CLI integration tests must isolate storage from real user directories by mocking `fs` or overriding `os.homedir()` with a temporary test directory. This guarantees tests are reproducible and clean up after themselves.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Test Suite for StorageEngine
In `tests/storage.test.ts`:
```typescript
import { describe, it, expect, beforeEach, vi } from "vitest";
import { StorageEngine, StoredTask } from "../src/storage/db";
import fs from "fs";

vi.mock("fs");

describe("StorageEngine Unit Tests", () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    vi.clearAllMocks();
  });

  it("should parse tasks from JSON storage", () => {
    const mockTasks: StoredTask[] = [
      { id: "task-1", title: "Write tests", priority: "HIGH", tags: [], completed: false, createdAt: new Date().toISOString() }
    ];
    vi.spyOn(fs, "existsSync").mockReturnValue(true);
    vi.spyOn(fs, "readFileSync").mockReturnValue(JSON.stringify(mockTasks));

    const tasks = StorageEngine.getTasks();
    expect(tasks).toHaveLength(1);
    expect(tasks[0].title).toBe("Write tests");
  });
