# Phase 4 — Fault Tolerance: Exponential Backoff Retries & Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)

## 🎯 What You Will Accomplish

Build enterprise fault tolerance into task execution. Implement exponential backoff retries with full randomized jitter to prevent the thundering herd problem. When retries are exhausted, quarantine failed tasks into a Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) with complete stack traces.

---

## 💡 Core Concepts & Architecture Decisions

When a downstream service suffers an outage, simultaneous retries with fixed delays hit the server in waves (thundering herd). Full jitter calculates randomized delay: `sleep = rand(0, min(maxBackoff, baseBackoff * 2^attempt))`, smoothing retry traffic across the time window.

---

## 🛠 Step-by-Step Implementation

### Step 1 · Custom Jittered Backoff Strategy
In `src/utils/backoff.ts`:
```typescript
export function calculateFullJitterDelay(attempt: number, baseMs = 1000, maxMs = 30000): number {
  const exponential = Math.min(maxMs, baseMs * Math.pow(2, attempt));
  return Math.floor(Math.random() * exponential);
}
```

### Step 2 · Route Exhausted Jobs to DLQ
In `src/workers/workflowWorker.ts`:
```typescript
import { Queue } from "bullmq";
import { redisConnection } from "../lib/redis";
import { prisma } from "../lib/prisma";

export const dlqQueue = new Queue("workflow-dlq", { connection: redisConnection });

workflowWorker.on("failed", async (job, err) => {
  if (!job) return;
  console.error(`❌ Job ${job.id} failed attempt ${job.attemptsMade}: ${err.message}`);

  if (job.attemptsMade >= (job.opts.attempts || 3)) {
    console.warn(`🚨 Job ${job.id} exhausted retries. Routing to Dead Letter Queue (DLQ).`);

    await dlqQueue.add("dead-job", {
      originalJobId: job.id,
      data: job.data,
      error: err.message,
      failedAt: new Date().toISOString()
    });

    await prisma.workflowStep.update({
      where: { id: job.data.stepId },
      data: { status: "FAILED", errorMessage: err.message }
    });
  }
});
```

